    "https://recruiting.ultipro.com/MON1000MON/JobBoard/d4da5ea7-24db-4f02-a484-7497ffffb76d/OpportunityDetail?opportunityId=eaff85a5-eb5f-4d07-a917-967bb20680c3": {
      "title": "Adjunct, French",
      "description": "Adjunct, French. Monmouth University is seeking applications for an Adjunct Professor of French in the World Languages and Cultures Department. Part-time Adjunct Faculty members typically teach 3-6 credits per semester, with class schedules determined based on departmental needs and candidate availability. Courses are taught in person at the West Long Branch campus. Adjunct positions remain posted continuously to expand the university’s pool of qualified adjunct faculty. Screening of applications will commence when an open position becomes available. Should a position become available, you will receive an email advising you of the next steps. This is an in-person, on-campus, non-remote position. For additional information about the department, please visit the Department of World Languages and Cultures webpage. Additional Application Material Required: Monmouth University requires all applications and supporting documents to be submitted via the university’s career portal. In addition to the application, candidates must upload the following documents. Applications will not be considered complete for review until all required documents are uploaded. Resume or Curriculum Vitae Cover Letter Contact Information for Three Professional References Two Letters of Recommendation Optional Documents: None Special Instructions to Applicants: We encourage candidates to include information in the cover letter that addresses their approach to teaching and their plans to advance diversity, equity, and inclusion in their position at Monmouth University. Duties and Responsibilities: Teach 3-6 credits during the semester. Develop and implement active, engaging and communicative class lessons, incorporating a variety of activities in accordance with the curriculum and learning objectives. Hold office hours (1 hour per 3-credit class) during the week to meet with students outside of class. Foster a positive and inclusive learning environment conducive to student engagement and academic success. Develop and administer projects, exams and other assessments to evaluate student learning outcomes and provide grades in a timely manner. Provide timely feedback and guidance to students to support their learning and development. Work with course coordinator as needed to ensure consistency on course syllabus, classroom approaches and assessments. Enrichment Statement: Monmouth University values diversity and invites applications from underrepresented groups who will enrich the teaching and service missions of the university. Minimum Qualifications: Master’s degree or higher in French or a Master of Arts in Teaching degree with a concentration in French. Previous experience in teaching classes of French. Candidates should be familiar and experienced in the communicative method of language teaching, focusing on student-centered learning. Familiarity with World Language technologies and online learning platforms is essential. Preferred Qualifications: None Questions regarding this search should be directed to: Julia Riordan-Goncalves at jriordan@monmouth.edu or 732-263-5808 Note to Applicants: Adjunct positions remain posted continuously to expand the university’s pool of qualified adjunct faculty. Screening of applications will commence when an open position becomes available. Should a position become available, you will receive an email advising you of the next steps. Working at Monmouth University perks: Employee Assistance Program (EAP) Employee Tuition Remission Employee elective deferrals to TIAA, 403(b) plan On campus, Fitness Center – free membership To view a full list of benefits, visit our benefits page at: Employee Benefits Information | Human Resources | Monmouth University Department: World Languages Work Schedule: varies Total Weeks Per Year: 14 Expected Salary: $1,100 per/credit Union: N/A Job Posting Close Date N/A"
    },
    "https://recruiting.ultipro.com/MON1000MON/JobBoard/d4da5ea7-24db-4f02-a484-7497ffffb76d/OpportunityDetail?opportunityId=14b20429-ba69-42d9-aa22-a1d424397b39": {
      "title": "University Clinician",
      "description": "University Clinician. Monmouth University is seeking applications for a University Clinician in the Health Services department. The candidate would be expected to: conduct medical evaluation of patients seeking care at the Health Center; patient population to include students, employees and spouses/partners of employees; perform either a complete or focused physical assessment; formulate medical diagnosis and order and implement a treatment plan; prescribe medications within scope of Collaborative Agreement; order diagnostic tests as needed; follow-up on treatment plan and document in the medical record. This is an in-person, on-campus, non-remote position. Duties and Responsibilities: Order and administer immunizations, provide intradermal TB skin testing, allergy injections. Order and administer oral medications, intramuscular, subcutaneous and if needed, IV medications/fluids. Order and administer oxygen and aerosol treatments. Collaborate with consulting physician to establish a Collaborative Practice Agreement. Collaborate with consulting physician on complex medical cases as needed. Conduct professional referrals both within and outside of the University setting including internal referrals to Counseling and Psychological Services. Collaborates with athletic trainers regarding medical care of student athletes. Conduct in-house specimen collection and CLIA – approved diagnostic testing such as rapid strep, mono, influenza, pregnancy. Order and draw bloodwork. Spin down necessary blood tubes and prepare lab specimens. Enter lab orders into online lab system. Conduct medical evaluation of patients seeking care at the Health Center. Patient population to include students, employees and spouses/partners of employees. Perform either a complete or focused physical assessment. Formulate medical diagnosis and order and implement a treatment plan. Prescribe medications within scope of Collaborative Agreement. Order diagnostic tests as needed. Follow-up on treatment plan and document in the medical record. Provide a range of women’s health services including annual PAP testing, interventional gyn exams, STD screenings/treatment, breast exams and prescription of birth control pills and emergency contraception. Provide a range of men’s health services including STD screenings and treatment. Provide customized medical care, health counseling and education for a culturally diverse population including international students and LGBTQ students/employees. Assesses diverse groups for health-related risk factors and engages the patient in thoughtful discussion about how to reduce risk. Maintain confidentiality and privacy of medical information. Provide treatment in accordance with approved Collaborative Practice Agreement and within the scope and practice of NJ licensure parameters for physician assistant’s or advance practice NP’s. Responsible for the triage, assessment, medical diagnosis and treatment of students and employees who present with illness, injury and psychological needs to the university Health Center. Provide routine physical assessments and other wellness exams and screenings. Collaborates with Office of Compliance to screen employees for OSHA respirator fit testing and makes referrals to Occupational Health when appropriate. Review laboratory and diagnostic findings; conduct patient follow-up calls; track and monitor all lab results. Serve as preceptor to University PA or APN students; providing instruction and oversight. Collaborate with PA or Nursing faculty to provide written feedback on clinical student performance. In the event of a medical emergency, initiate BLS measures and administer oxygen, epinephrine and other medications as appropriate to the situation including IV fluids. Organize and/or participate in health promotion initiatives on campus. Collaborate with other University departments as needed to implement health initiatives. Navigate the electronic medical record system to document subjective and objective data including physical assessment. Select appropriate diagnosis codes and document treatment plan including prescribed medications, diagnostics/labs treatments, referrals and follow-up care. Minimum Qualifications: Physician Assistant Master’s Degree and current New Jersey Physician Assistant license OR a Master of Science in Nursing ( MSN ) degree with Nurse Practitioner specialization, with National certification as a Nurse Practitioner and licensed by State of NJ as a Adult Nurse Practitioner ( ANP ) or Family Nurse Practitioner ( FNP ). One year (or equivalent) clinical experience working as a PA or APN in a healthcare setting. Cardiopulmonary Resuscitation ( CPR ): Basic Lifesaving Skills ( BLS ) for Healthcare Providers certification, or obtain within 3 months of date of hire. Strong physical assessment, diagnostic and treatment skills. Sensitivity to diversity and confidentiality. Ability to collaborate as a vital member of the healthcare team. Excellent interpersonal, organizational and communication skills. Must be able to treat confidential and sensitive information appropriately. Preferred Qualifications: Ability to navigate and accurately document in an electronic health record system; laboratory systems. Additional Application Material Required: Monmouth University requires all applications and supporting documents to be submitted via the university’s career portal. In addition to the application, candidates should upload the following: Resume or Curriculum Vitae Optional Documents: Cover Letter Professional References Questions regarding this search should be directed to: Barbara Santos at bsantos@monmouth.edu or 732-263-5629 Note to Applicants: This is a 44 week position with an anticipated start date of August 1, 2025. Please keep in mind that a job posted as “Open until filled” may close at any time without notice. As such, we encourage you to submit your application and all required documentation as quickly as possible to ensure consideration for the posted position. Working at Monmouth University perks: 403(b) Retirement Plan Generous Paid Time Off Medical, Dental & Vision Insurance Benefits Tuition Remission for employee upon hire Tuition Remission for spouse or civil-union partner and IRS dependent after one-year of full-time continuous employment Generous Paid Time Off Employer paid Short & Long-Term Disability Employer sponsored Life Insurance Employee Assistance Program (EAP), FSA, Telehealth and more To view a full list of benefits, visit our benefits page at: Employee Benefits Information | Human Resources | Monmouth University Department: Health Services Work Schedule: Monday through Friday Total Weeks Per Year 44 Hours Per Week: 36.25 Expected Salary: $80,000 - $85,000 Indicate salary Union: N/A Job Posting Close Date Open until filled"
    },
    "https://recruiting.ultipro.com/MON1000MON/JobBoard/d4da5ea7-24db-4f02-a484-7497ffffb76d/OpportunityDetail?opportunityId=cdb35a97-5267-40df-b27c-d1fb9526eb3d": {
      "title": "On-Call University Nurse Practitioner",
      "description": "On-Call University Nurse Practitioner. Monmouth University is seeking applications for On-Call Nurse Practitioners in the Health Center. The candidate would be expected to: conduct patient assessment, diagnosis and treatment for patients presenting to the Health Center with illness or injury; treatment is in accordance with evidence-based practice; within the scope of licensure for N.J. advanced practice nurses and in conjunction with Joint Protocols of Practice; perform specimen collection including phlebotomy and conduct CLIA -waived testing for samples collected; order diagnostic tests as appropriate to presenting condition; review, evaluate and provide treatment based upon diagnostic findings. This is an in-person, on-campus, non-remote position. Duties and Responsibilities: Conduct patient assessment, diagnosis and treatment for patients presenting to the Health Center with illness or injury. Treatment is in accordance with evidence-based practice; within the scope of licensure for N.J. advanced practice nurses and in conjunction with Joint Protocols of Practice. Perform specimen collection including phlebotomy and conduct CLIA -waived testing for samples collected. Order diagnostic tests as appropriate to presenting condition. Review, evaluate and provide treatment based upon diagnostic findings. Provide preventive immunizations, allergy injections and screenings for tuberculosis. Provide women’s services to include gynecological examinations, pregnancy and STI testing and counseling and prescribe birth control options. Other duties as assigned. Minimum Qualifications: Master of Science in Nursing (MSN) Current NJ licensure as a Registered Nurse (RN) Current NJ licensure as an Advanced Practice Nurse (APN) Clinical experience in an ambulatory or similar setting with an adolescent and/or adult population Ability to diagnose and prescribe for common, acute episodic illness and injury Excellent interpersonal, organizational and communication skills Must be able to treat confidential and sensitive information appropriately Preferred Qualifications: None Additional Application Material Required: Monmouth University requires all applications and supporting documents to be submitted via the university’s career portal. In addition to the application, candidates should upload the following: Resume or Curriculum Vitae Optional Documents: Cover Letter Professional References Questions regarding this search should be directed to: Barbara Santos at bsantos@monmouth.edu or 732-263-5629 Note to Applicants: On-call NP’s may be called to work during the academic semester weeks, September to May; M-F from 8:45 am to 5pm; no weekends. Please keep in mind that a job posted as “Open until filled” may close at any time without notice. As such, we encourage you to submit your application and all required documentation as quickly as possible to ensure consideration for the posted position. Working at Monmouth University perks: Earned sick leave: Monmouth University provides its part-time staff employees, including on-call staff and temporary employees, with earned sick leave according to the New Jersey Paid Sick Leave Act . Fitness Center - Employees are entitled to use the Fitness Center while employed in their PT or on-call capacity Recreational & Cultural Activities - Athletic and cultural events throughout the academic year are open to employees. Many of these events are open to employees free of charge or at reduced costs University Bookstore discount - Employees receive a ten percent (10%) discount on most items purchased in the University Bookstore. Department: Health Services Work Schedule: Monday through Friday Total Weeks Per Year: 28 weeks Hours Per Week: 26 hours Expected Salary: $55.00 per hour Union: N/A Job Posting Close Date Open until filled"
    },
    "https://recruiting.ultipro.com/MON1000MON/JobBoard/d4da5ea7-24db-4f02-a484-7497ffffb76d/OpportunityDetail?opportunityId=1595029f-ef5a-47a4-9cea-ba0e08c41a24": {
      "title": "Department Chair of Professional Counseling",
      "description": "Department Chair of Professional Counseling. Monmouth University invites applications for a full-time open-rank faculty appointment to serve as Department Chair of Professional Counseling within the Wayne D. McMurray School of Humanities and Social Sciences. This is a 12-month position. The anticipated start date is July 1, 2027 for an initial five-year term as Department Chair, subject to review and renewal. This is an in-person, on-campus, non-remote position. The Department of Professional Counseling offers a Master of Science in Clinical Mental Health Counseling , which aims to develop mental health professionals who can effectively address issues of wellness, personal growth, and pathology for the benefit of a diverse population. This 60-credit program, which prepares students for NJ licensure as Licensed Professional Counselors (LPC), is accredited by the Council for Accreditation of Counseling and Related Educational Programs (CACREP). This program satisfies academic requirements for the NJ-State licenses in LPC , with additional opportunities for students to obtain LCADC . Graduates of the Department are expected to contribute to and be leaders in their field through practice, education, and professional excellence. For additional information about the Professional Counseling Department, please visit the Department Website . Additional Application Material Required: Monmouth University requires all applications and supporting documents to be submitted via the university’s career portal. In addition to the application, candidates must upload the following documents. Applications will not be considered complete for review until all required documents are uploaded. Resume or Curriculum Vitae Cover Letter (addressing at least the minimum qualifications of the job, leadership philosophy and experience, teaching philosophy, and scholarly agenda) Contact Information for Three Professional References Scanned copy of independent professional counseling license from current state Optional Documents: Up to Three Letters of Recommendation Other: Any additional evidence the candidate wishes to include, such as teaching effectiveness and student mentoring Applications received by December 31, 2026 will receive full consideration. This position remains open until filled. Minimum Qualifications: Doctorate in counselor education and supervision with focus in mental health from a CACREP-accredited program. Licensed Professional Counselor in current state and LPC-eligible in the State of New Jersey Five (5) years teaching experience in a full-time tenure-track academic appointment with teaching responsibilities at the graduate level and demonstrated effectiveness teaching in-person graduate-level courses Demonstrated scholarly productivity in the field commensurate with the rank of Associate or Full Professor (advanced assistant professors with sufficient scholarship for promotion may also apply) Demonstrated record in academic leadership, including experience with budgeting and resource allocation, academic program assessment, as well as hiring, developing, and supervising faculty and staff in a student-centered educational environment Experience in supervision of curriculum Demonstrated capacity to mentor faculty and develop talent; and the ability to serve as an effective advocate for excellence in teaching, service, and scholarly activity in a collective bargaining environment. Demonstrated ability to collaborate effectively with faculty and lead group initiatives within and across departments. Demonstrated knowledge of Council for Accreditation of Counseling and Related Educational Programs (CACREP) standards Demonstrated success in program assessment and development Preferred Qualifications: Experience in supervision of clinical field placement personnel Academic or research interest in one of our specialty areas: Ecotherapy, spirituality, children/adolescents, alcohol and drug counseling (LCADC), or couples and relationship counseling Experience supporting students in academic and clinical remediation Experience with membership and/or leadership in ACA (and/or its divisions) and/or AMHCA Duties and Responsibilities: Effectively lead the Professional Counseling Department, including administration, fiscal management, continuous review and analysis, planning, program development and execution, accreditation, faculty hiring, faculty scheduling, and coordinating daily operations. Oversee maintaining accreditation through the Council for Accreditation of Counseling and Related Educational Programs (CACREP) Actively contribute to and lead faculty and staff in preparing data for maintaining accreditation through the Council for Accreditation of Counseling and Related Educational Programs (CACREP) Author and disseminate accreditation reports per accreditation requirements Actively contribute to and lead faculty and staff in program assessment, outcomes analysis, and continuous quality improvement activities, including preparation for internal and external program reviews Oversee faculty hiring; and supervise, mentor, and evaluate faculty and staff to facilitate excellence in teaching, scholarship, and service based on position requirements Engage in scholarly activities and service to the University, School, program, profession, and community Cultivate excellence in research/scholarship, teaching, and service and help to engage faculty and students in collaborative research and scholarly experiences Manage faculty in overseeing student progress, advising, professional behavior expectations, and complex student issues in collaboration with faculty and administration Teach 3 credits of graduate courses per semester (12 credits with a 3-credit course load reduction for scholarship and a 6-credit course load reduction for Chair position). Maintain disciplinary currency as defined in the Professional Counseling Department scholarship statement and the current MU-FAMCO Agreement and contribute to the department’s scholarship agenda Submit an annual report to the School Dean, Associate Provost, and Provost Other duties as assigned Questions regarding this search should be directed to: Dr. Kristin Bluemel, Associate Dean of the School of Humanities and Social Sciences, at kbluemel@monmouth.edu Note to Applicants: Please keep in mind that a job posted as “Open until filled” may close at any time without notice. As such, we encourage you to submit your application and all required documentation as quickly as possible to ensure consideration for the posted position. Working at Monmouth University perks – MU offers: 403(b) Retirement Plan (with employer contribution) Generous Paid Time Off Medical, Dental & Vision Insurance Benefits Tuition Remission for employee upon hire Tuition Remission for spouse or civil-union partner and IRS dependent after one-year of full-time continuous employment Employer paid Short & Long-Term Disability Employer sponsored Life Insurance Employee Assistance Program (EAP), FSA, Telehealth and more To view a full list of benefits, visit our benefits page at: Employee Benefits Information | Human Resources | Monmouth University Department: Professional Counseling, Wayne D. McMurray School of Humanities and Social Sciences Work Schedule: Varies Total Weeks Per Year 52 Hours Per Week: 36.25 Expected Salary: Faculty Base Salary of $94,500 per CBA; and a $10,000 annual stipend and course release time of 12 credits per academic year to serve as Department Chair per the Roles and Responsibilities of Chairs and Academic Departments at Monmouth University document. Union: N/A Job Posting Close Date: Open until filled"
    },
    "https://recruiting.ultipro.com/MON1000MON/JobBoard/d4da5ea7-24db-4f02-a484-7497ffffb76d/OpportunityDetail?opportunityId=ff2cef1b-9e8a-4b99-89c0-1fe9312e30d8": {
      "title": "Adjunct, American Sign Language (ASL)",
      "description": "Adjunct, American Sign Language (ASL). The Communication department at Monmouth University is seeking applications for Adjunct Professors for American Sign Language (ASL). Part-time Adjunct Faculty members typically teach 3-6 credits per semester, with class schedules determined based on departmental needs and candidate availability. Courses are taught in person at the West Long Branch campus. Adjunct positions remain posted continuously to expand the university’s pool of qualified adjunct faculty. Screening of applications will commence when an open position becomes available. Should a position become available, you will receive an email advising you of the next steps. This is an in-person, on-campus, non-remote position. For additional information about the department, please visit the Department of Communication webpage. Additional Application Material Required: Monmouth University requires all applications and supporting documents to be submitted via the university’s career portal. In addition to the application, candidates must upload the following documents. Applications will not be considered complete for review until all required documents are uploaded. Resume or Curriculum Vitae Cover Letter Contact Information for Three Professional References Two Letters of Recommendation Optional Documents: None Special Instructions to Applicants: We encourage candidates to include information in the cover letter that addresses their approach to teaching and their plans to advance diversity, equity, and inclusion in their position at Monmouth University. Duties and Responsibilities: Teach 3-6 credits during the semester. Develop and deliver course lectures, discussions and assignments in accordance with the curriculum and learning objectives. Provide time during the week to meet with students outside of class. Foster a positive and inclusive learning environment conducive to student engagement and academic success. Develop and administer projects and exams to evaluate student learning outcomes and provide grades in a timely manner. Provide timely feedback and guidance to students to support their learning and development. Enrichment Statement: Monmouth University values diversity and invites applications from underrepresented groups who will enrich the teaching and service missions of the university. Minimum Qualifications: Bachelor’s degree. Fluency in American Sign Language (ASL). One year of experience teaching or interpreting ASL. Familiarity with deaf culture. Excellent interpersonal, organizational and communication skills. Preferred Qualifications: RID Certification Questions regarding this search should be directed to: Deanna Shoemaker at dshoemak@monmouth.edu or 732-263-5194 Note to Applicants: Adjunct positions remain posted continuously to expand the university’s pool of qualified adjunct faculty. Screening of applications will commence when an open position becomes available. Should a position become available, you will receive an email advising you of the next steps. Working at Monmouth University perks: Employee Assistance Program (EAP) Employee Tuition Remission Employee elective deferrals to TIAA, 403(b) plan On campus, Fitness Center – free membership To view a full list of benefits, visit our benefits page at: Employee Benefits Information | Human Resources | Monmouth University Department: Communication Work Schedule: varies Total Weeks Per Year: 14 Expected Salary: $1,100 per credit Union: N/A Job Posting Close Date N/A"
    },
    "https://recruiting.ultipro.com/MON1000MON/JobBoard/d4da5ea7-24db-4f02-a484-7497ffffb76d/OpportunityDetail?opportunityId=60a55bf7-2f86-4562-87b0-92456d80a45e": {
      "title": "Associate Athletic Director for Facilities and Event Management",
      "description": "Associate Athletic Director for Facilities and Event Management. Monmouth University is seeking an experienced professional for the position of Associate Athletic Director for Facilities and Event Management within the Department of Athletics. The Associate Athletic Director for Facilities and Event Management provides administrative oversight and operational direction for the planning, coordination, and execution of intercollegiate athletic events. This position oversees the effective and safe operation of athletic facilities and event venues, ensuring that competitions, practices, and special events are conducted in accordance with institutional policies, NCAA regulations, conference requirements, and applicable health, safety, and security standards. The Associate Athletic Director provides direct oversight of designated intercollegiate sports programs, working collaboratively with sport administrators, coaches, student-athletes, and departmental leadership to support competitive excellence, student-athlete development, operational efficiency, and compliance. The position coordinates event management functions, including facility readiness, staffing, game-day operations, security, crowd management, visiting team services, officials, and coordination with campus and external partners. In collaboration with internal and external stakeholders, the Associate Athletic Director establishes event management procedures, develops operational plans and budgets, evaluates event performance, and implements strategies to enhance the student-athlete, spectator, and campus community experience. This is an in-person, on-campus, non-remote position. For more information about Monmouth Athletics, please visit the Athletics webpage at https://monmouthhawks.com/ Additional Application Material Required: Monmouth University requires all applications and supporting documents to be submitted via the university's career portal. In addition to the application, candidates must upload the following documents. Applications will not be considered complete for review until all required documents are uploaded. Resume or Curriculum Vitae Cover Letter Professional References Duties and Responsibilities: Direct all operational planning, event management, and logistics for Monmouth University Athletic home events, ensuring a first-class experience for student-athletes, coaches, officials, and spectators. Oversee the recruitment, training, scheduling, supervision, and performance evaluation of event management student staff. Manage the event management budget, including payroll oversight, timesheet approval, expense monitoring, and fiscal accountability. Coordinate scheduling of departmental cars, golf carts, and repairs. Develop policies for hosting athletics events. Interpret coaching requests and plan accordingly for home events based on budget and facility limitations. Manage all requests and activities for all officials and visiting teams for all home games. Coordinate bid submissions, planning, and hosting of Coastal Athletic Association (CAA), Northeast Conference (NEC), and NCAA postseason events for assigned sports. Serve as the primary game-day administrator for athletic event technology systems, including DVSport ,Spiideo, Daktronics, and other competition and venue technologies. Develop, implement, and maintain comprehensive event operations policies, procedures, and a student worker handbook. Responsible for adhering to policies and procedures governing the Department of Athletics and the University and any applicable rules and regulations associated with the National Collegiate Athletic Association (NCAA), the Coastal Athletic Associate (CAA) and Big South conference or any other conference or organization with which the University is or becomes associated with which affects University Athletics. Plan personnel and equipment requirements for all home events. Provide support for athletic facilities and operations initiatives, including facility readiness, maintenance coordination, and operational projects. Serve as the primary liaison with Campus Police, Facilities Management, and other campus partners. Support emergency action planning, crowd management, and game-day risk management efforts for all assigned events. Coordinate with Conference and Event Services to support campus-wide events. Serve as the primary events liaison with the CAA and NEC on event management and championship operational matters. Manage relationships with vendors, contractors, official, and external service providers to support athletic events and facilities operations. Serve as the Sport Administrator for assigned varsity programs, providing administrative oversight and support to coaches and student-athletes. Supervision of the Hackensack Meridian Health Athletic Center as assigned. Other duties as assigned. Minimum Qualifications: Bachelor's degree. Experience as an events coordinator and/or and with and knowledge of repairing equipment. Ability to work evenings, weekends, and other non-traditional hours as required. A valid driver’s license in your state of residence and have had no serious violations (Type A) within the past five (5) years and no more than three (3) moving violations (Type B) in the past three (3) years. University Policy found here: Monmouth University Vehicle Use Policy . Excellent interpersonal, organizational, and communication skills. Ability to appropriately handle confidential and sensitive information. Understanding of the University's commitment to academic success and athletic excellence. Basic proficiency with Microsoft Word and Microsoft Excel. Ability to lift and move heavy objects up to 60lbs on a regular basis. Demonstrated ability to manage multiple assignments and priorities simultaneously. Preferred Qualifications: Three years experience as an events coordinator and/or and with and knowledge of repairing equipment. Familiarity with EMS scheduling software and Teamworks Supervisory experience, including the management of full-time, part time, interns, and/or student staff. Demonstrated experience coordinating large-scale athletic events, conference championships, and postseason competitions. Experience as a collegiate student-athlete, student manager, graduate assistant, or athletics administrator preferred. Note to Applicants: Please keep in mind that a job posted as “Open until filled” may close at any time without notice. As such, we encourage you to submit your application and all required documentation as quickly as possible to ensure consideration for the posted position. Working at Monmouth University perks: 403(b) Retirement Plan Generous Paid Time Off Medical, Dental & Vision Insurance Benefits Tuition Remission for employee upon hire Tuition Remission for spouse or civil-union partner and IRS dependent after one year of full-time continuous employment Employer-paid Short & Long-Term Disability Employer-sponsored Life Insurance Employee Assistance Program (EAP), FSA, Telehealth and more Department: Athletics Work Schedule: Must be available to work weekends, evenings, and other hours as required by athletic events and operations. Total Weeks Per Year: 52 Hours Per Week: 36.25 or as needed by the Department Expected Salary: $80,000 - $83,000 Union: N/A Job Posting Close Date: Open until filled"
    },
    "https://recruiting.ultipro.com/MON1000MON/JobBoard/d4da5ea7-24db-4f02-a484-7497ffffb76d/OpportunityDetail?opportunityId=1e257de2-9ca2-47f7-98d0-7c074191d648": {
      "title": "On-Call Vessel Captain",
      "description": "On-Call Vessel Captain. Monmouth University has an immediate opening for an On-Call Vessel Captain within the School of Science. The position will support efforts of the Marine Environmental Biology and Policy program, Urban Coast Institute, as well as other University constituencies and partners. The incumbent will operate and maintain the Universities vessels 14-50ft. Large vessel is docked in Atlantic Highlands NJ and small boats are trailered out of campus. Provide support for student training and academic research, as well as contract research efforts in fisheries, sediment sampling, water quality, moorings and hydrographic survey efforts. The position will require USCG 100ton License, abilities for precise vessel maneuvering, occasional nighttime work, and deployment of a variety of oceanographic equipment and fishing gears. Other duties include vessel maintenance and accommodating research equipment loading, hookup, and deployment. Cruises typically range from one to four hours in length for student training with 8 to 12 hour days for research and contract work. The position reports to the Dean of the School of Science. The ideal candidate will work well in a dynamic environment with scientists, as well as college students from many different disciplines, including marine biology, fisheries ecology, and oceanography. A description of the vessel fleet and associated equipment can be found here . This is an in-person, on-campus, non-remote position. For additional information about the School, please visit the School of Science webpage. Additional Application Material Required: Monmouth University requires all applications and supporting documents to be submitted via the university’s career portal. In addition to the application, candidates must upload the following documents. Applications will not be considered complete for review until all required documents are uploaded. Resume or Curriculum Vitae Cover Letter Optional Documents: Professional References Duties and Responsibilities: Oversee operation, maintenance, servicing and repair of University vessels, vehicles and trailers. Other duties as assigned Minimum Qualifications: Experience serving as a captain or senior crew member on a research, commercial, or military vessel, including twin and single engine propulsion vessels. Hold a valid US Coast Guard Master- Near Coastal Waters, 100-ton license. Hold a valid Transportation Worker Identification Card ( TWIC ) credential. Hold a valid First Aid, CPR , and AED training certification. A valid driver’s license in your state of residence and have had no serious violations (Type A) within the past five (5) years and no more than three (3) moving violations (Type B) in the past three (3) years. University Policy found here: Monmouth University Vehicle Use Policy . Hold a valid Standards of Training and Certification of Keeping Watch ( STCW ) basic credential or obtain within 6 months of hire. Possess demonstrable vessel trailering capabilities with vessels up to 30 feet. Physical ability to conduct field work under a variety of weather conditions and moving decks. Physical ability to ascend and descend ladders to 10ft, and ability to lift, carry, or move objects up to 50 lbs. Ability to work evenings, weekends, and extended hours as needed. Preferred Qualifications: Experience navigating coastal waters of NJ, including NY Harbor, Barnegat Bay and adjacent nearshore ocean waters Experience with operation and maintenance of marine mechanical systems, hydraulics, power tools, and vessel safety equipment Experience in marine/oceanographic instrumentation applications and use (i.e., calibration and operation of data sondes and other water quality monitoring devices, benthic sampling, plankton sampling and fishery techniques) Familiarity with Hydrographic and GPS survey techniques Working knowledge of Geographic Information System ( GIS ) Knowledge of Microsoft applications, Windows operating system (Excel, Word, PowerPoint) Knowledge of device interfacing and application software specific to sampling, analysis and positioning i.e., Hypack Experience in bottom and mid-depth trawling Questions regarding this search should be directed to: Joe Coyle, Dean jcoyle@monmouth.edu or 732 571-3421 Note to Applicants: Please keep in mind that a job posted as “Open until filled” may close at any time without notice. As such, we encourage you to submit your application and all required documentation as quickly as possible to ensure consideration for the posted position. Working at Monmouth University perks: Earned sick leave: Monmouth University provides its part-time staff employees, including on-call staff and temporary employees, with earned sick leave according to the New Jersey Paid Sick Leave Act . Recreational & Cultural Activities - Athletic and cultural events throughout the academic year are open to employees. Many of these events are open to employees free of charge or at reduced costs University Bookstore discount - Employees receive a ten percent (10%) discount on most items purchased in the University Bookstore. Department: School of Science Work Schedule: Monday through Friday Total Weeks Per Year: 15 to 20 Hours Per Week: Up to 24 hours Expected Salary: $45.00 - $50.00 p/hour Union: N/A Job Posting Close Date: Open until filled"
    },
    "https://recruiting.ultipro.com/MON1000MON/JobBoard/d4da5ea7-24db-4f02-a484-7497ffffb76d/OpportunityDetail?opportunityId=24d567c5-0b74-4b31-9a5a-99e90fdb2e31": {
      "title": "Athletics Facilities and Operations Assistant - Part-Time",
      "description": "Athletics Facilities and Operations Assistant - Part-Time. The Athletics Facilities and Operations Assistant supports the daily operation of athletic and recreational facilities and assists with athletic events and departmental programs. This position helps ensure facilities are safe, functional, and prepared to support practices, competitions, special events, and other departmental activities. The role provides operational support for facility and event management, including setup and breakdown, customer service, and game-day operations, while fostering a positive experience for students, staff, coaches, spectators, and campus visitors. This is an in-person, on-campus, non-remote position. For more information about Monmouth Athletics, please visit the Athletics webpage at https://monmouthhawks.com/ Additional Application Material Required: Monmouth University requires all applications and supporting documents to be submitted via the university's career portal. In addition to the application, candidates must upload the following documents. Applications will not be considered complete for review until all required documents are uploaded. Resume or Curriculum Vitae Cover Letter Optional: Professional References Duties and Responsibilities: Assist with daily operations, including safety standards, cleanliness, equipment placement, and facility upkeep of the Fitness Center Responsible for adhering to policies and procedures governing the Department of Athletics, the University and any applicable rules and regulations associated with the National Collegiate Athletic Association (NCAA), the Coastal Athletic Association, Northeast Conference (NEC), or any other conference or organization with which the University is or becomes associated with which affects University Athletics. Monitor equipment inventory, order supplies, and coordinate maintenance and repairs Enforce policies to protect equipment, control facility access, and maintain a safe workout environment in the Hackensack Meridian Health Athletic Center (HMAC) and other assigned Monmouth University facilities Manage, train, and schedule student employees Serve as the member-facing point of contact: respond to inquiries, resolve service concerns, and support retention Monitor health, safety, and equipment standards (CPR/AED current; maintain certifications and logs) Conduct routine facility inspections and communicate operational needs to the appropriate staff. Maintain required documentation, incident logs, and regulatory or institutional safety/compliance records Support preparation, setup, and breakdown of athletics facilities for practices, competitions, rentals, and special events. Other duties as assigned. Minimum Qualifications: High school diploma or equivalent. Experience in recreation, fitness center operations, athletic facilities, event operations, or a related setting. Strong communication, organizational, and customer service skills. Ability to work independently, exercise sound judgment, and collaborate with staff and student employees. Availability to work a flexible schedule, including evenings and weekends. Current CPR/AED and First Aid certification, or ability to obtain certification within a University-designated timeframe. Preferred Qualifications: Bachelor’s degree in sport management, recreation, exercise science, or a related field. Experience working in a college or university setting. Experience supervising student employees and supporting athletic events. Note to Applicants: Please keep in mind that a job posted as “Open until filled” may close at any time without notice. As such, we encourage you to submit your application and all required documentation as quickly as possible to ensure consideration for the posted position. Working at Monmouth University perks: Part-Time Staff earn sick leave according to the New Jersey Paid Sick Leave Act . Employee Assistance Program (EAP). Fitness Center - Employees are entitled to use the Fitness Center while employed in their PT capacity. Recreational & Cultural Activities - Athletic and cultural events throughout the academic year are open to employees. Many of these events are open to employees free of charge or at reduced costs. University Bookstore discount - Employees receive a ten percent (10%) discount on most items purchased in the University Bookstore. To view a full list of benefits, visit our benefits page at: Employee Benefits Information | Human Resources | Monmouth University Department: Athletics Work Schedule: TDB Total Weeks Per Year: 52 Hours Per Week: 20 - 24 Expected Salary: $22 per hour Union: N/A Job Posting Close Date: Open until filled"
    }
  },
